### 1. Modify vm.h

Replace the original globals hash table with:

```c
Table globalNames;
ValueArray globalValues;
```

The globalNames table maps variable names to their
corresponding indexes in globalValues.

The globalValues array stores the actual values
of the global variables.

For example, the declaration:

```lox
var a = "value";
```

Would produce an entry similar to:

globalNames: "a" -> 0
globalValues[0]: "value"

### 2. Modify compiler.c

Update identifierConstant() so it finds or assigns
a global variable's array index during compilation.

```c
static uint8_t identifierConstant(Token* name) {
  Value index;

  ObjString* identifier =
      copyString(name->start, name->length);

  if (tableGet(&vm.globalNames, identifier, &index)) {
    return (uint8_t)AS_NUMBER(index);
  }

  uint8_t newIndex = (uint8_t)vm.globalValues.count;

  writeValueArray(&vm.globalValues, UNDEFINED_VAL);

  tableSet(&vm.globalNames, identifier,
           NUMBER_VAL((double)newIndex));

  return newIndex;
}
```

This function first checks whether the variable name
already has an assigned index.

If it does, the existing index is returned.

Otherwise, a new slot is created in the globalValues
array, and its index is recorded in globalNames.

### 3. Add an Undefined Value Type

The interpreter needs a way to distinguish between
a global variable that has been defined and one
that has only been assigned a slot.

We cannot use nil for this because nil is a valid
value in Lox.

Therefore, the proposed solution introduces a new
internal value called UNDEFINED_VAL.

Conceptually, value.h would include:

```c
// Add to ValueType:
VAL_UNDEFINED

// Add the following macros:
#define IS_UNDEFINED(value) \
    ((value).type == VAL_UNDEFINED)

#define UNDEFINED_VAL \
    ((Value){VAL_UNDEFINED, {.number = 0}})
```

This special value marks a global variable slot
that has not been defined yet.

### 4. Modify vm.c – Reading Global Variables

Replace the runtime hash table lookup with direct
array indexing.

```c
case OP_GET_GLOBAL: {
  Value value = vm.globalValues.values[READ_BYTE()];

  if (IS_UNDEFINED(value)) {
    runtimeError("Undefined variable.");
    return INTERPRET_RUNTIME_ERROR;
  }

  push(value);
  break;
}
```

The VM reads the variable's index directly from
the bytecode and accesses its value in the array.

The undefined check preserves Lox's behavior when
a program tries to access a variable that has
not been defined.

### 5. Modify vm.c – Defining Global Variables

```c
case OP_DEFINE_GLOBAL: {
  vm.globalValues.values[READ_BYTE()] = pop();
  break;
}
```

Instead of storing the variable in a hash table,
the VM stores its value directly in the
corresponding array position.

### 6. Modify vm.c – Assigning Global Variables

```c
case OP_SET_GLOBAL: {
  uint8_t index = READ_BYTE();

  if (IS_UNDEFINED(vm.globalValues.values[index])) {
    runtimeError("Undefined variable.");
    return INTERPRET_RUNTIME_ERROR;
  }

  vm.globalValues.values[index] = peek(0);
  break;
}
```

This checks whether the variable was previously
defined before allowing assignment.

It also leaves the assigned value on the stack
because assignment is an expression in Lox.

### 7. Initialize and Free the New Structures

The VM must initialize the new table and array:

```c
initTable(&vm.globalNames);
initValueArray(&vm.globalValues);
```

When shutting down, the VM must free them:

```c
freeTable(&vm.globalNames);
freeValueArray(&vm.globalValues);
```

These changes replace the original initialization
and cleanup of vm.globals.

## Performance Comparison

### Original Implementation

Every global variable lookup requires the VM to
search the globals hash table using the variable name.

Even though hash table operations are approximately
O(1) on average, they still involve hashing,
comparing keys, and possibly resolving collisions.

### Optimized Implementation

The compiler determines each global variable's
array index ahead of time.

At runtime, the VM uses the stored index to access
the variable directly.

This eliminates repeated runtime hash table lookups
and reduces the work required to access globals.

## Preserving Lox Semantics

The optimization must still preserve important
language rules.

- Global variables can be defined after their names
  are first encountered during compilation.
- Accessing an undefined global must produce a
  runtime error.
- Assigning to an undefined global must also
  produce a runtime error.
- Global variables must persist between REPL inputs.
- Global variables can be redefined.

The UNDEFINED_VAL marker is necessary to distinguish
a reserved variable slot from an actual defined value.

